// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
//
// Expected: c1a1a0 — Needham-Schroeder public key (1978), the UNFIXED version:
// message 2 carries CONCAT(ni, nr) with no responder identity. This is
// Lowe's 1995 attack, found rather than assumed.
//
// The pair with spore_nsl_pk.vp is the point: that model is this one plus
// Lowe's fix, and it holds where this one falls.
//
// Peer instantiation is what makes the question askable at all. Lowe's
// attack needs an honest initiator running legitimately with a
// corrupt-but-legitimate peer while a concurrent run proceeds with an honest
// one, so a model with one fixed cast cannot state it, and --sessions does
// not help: every session replicates the same cast. The scenarios[] block at
// the bottom is that peer axis.
//
// Alice runs the protocol twice over: once with Bob, once with Mallory, who
// is a legitimate participant with a leaked private key. Verifpal finds:
//
//   1. the attacker takes ni@2 out of Alice's run with Mallory, reseals it
//      under Bob's key WITH ALICE'S OWN IDENTITY, and sends it to Bob;
//   2. Bob's identity check passes, because the identity really is Alice's;
//   3. Bob replies CONCAT(ni@2, nr), which the attacker relays to Alice@2;
//   4. Alice@2's nonce check passes, because ni@2 really is hers;
//   5. Alice@2 seals nr to Mallory's key, and the attacker opens it with mk.
//
// Bob finishes believing he spoke to Alice, and the attacker holds Bob's nr.
// Nothing was forged and no key was broken: every message is one an honest
// principal genuinely produced, delivered to the wrong run.
//
// Modelling note. Bob's `ASSERT(id_b, ga)?` is not an extra assumption. It is
// what "Bob's run with Alice as his peer" means operationally, and it is what
// SPDL gets for free from pattern matching: `recv_3(I,R,{Ni,I}pk(R))` with I
// instantiated to Alice only accepts messages carrying Alice's identity.
// Verifpal's receive is an assignment rather than a pattern, so the check is
// written out. Without it Bob has no peer at all and the query is not the one
// Lowe's attack is about.
//
// The two authentication queries are the peer axis read the other way round.
// m1 fails in both models, and legitimately: Mallory opens Alice@2's request
// with the private key she leaked and reseals it under Bob's, so Bob accepts a
// ciphertext no Alice run produced. Alice@2 is a run of Alice with a different
// peer binding, so it is not a run Bob's acceptance may be matched against,
// and widening the matching-run rule to every clone of a principal would lose
// exactly this attack. m2 holds in both: the attacker can route Alice's
// request into Bob's other scenario copy and bring that copy's reply back, but
// that copy is a run of the same Bob under the same peer binding — the
// scenario rebinds Alice, not Bob — and Alice accepts its reply once.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private a
	knows public gpeer
	ga = PUBKEY(a)
	generates ni
]

principal Bob[
	knows private b
	gb = PUBKEY(b)
	generates nr
]

principal Mallory[
	knows private mk
	gm = PUBKEY(mk)
	leaks mk
]

Alice -> Bob: [ga]
Bob -> Alice: [gb]
Mallory -> Alice: [gm]

principal Alice[
	m1 = PKE_ENC(gpeer, CONCAT(ni, ga))
]

Alice -> Bob: m1

principal Bob[
	d1 = PKE_DEC(b, m1)
	ni_b, id_b = SPLIT(d1)?
	_ = ASSERT(id_b, ga)?
	m2 = PKE_ENC(id_b, CONCAT(ni_b, nr))
]

Bob -> Alice: m2

principal Alice[
	d2 = PKE_DEC(a, m2)
	ni_a, nr_a = SPLIT(d2)?
	_ = ASSERT(ni_a, ni)?
	m3 = PKE_ENC(gpeer, nr_a)
]

Alice -> Bob: m3

principal Bob[
	d3 = PKE_DEC(b, m3)
	_ = ASSERT(d3, nr)?
]

scenarios[
	Alice[gpeer = gb]
	Alice[gpeer = gm]
]

queries[
	confidentiality? nr
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: m1
	authentication? Bob -> Alice: m2
]
